WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.200
Multimodal AI.

2
00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:07.900
One model working across text and images, and it all hinges on a shared embedding space.

3
00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:11.967
CLIP's insight is to train two encoders together.

4
00:00:12.267 --> 00:00:14.033
An image goes in one side.

5
00:00:14.333 --> 00:00:16.667
Its encoder maps it into a vector space.

6
00:00:16.967 --> 00:00:23.833
A text encoder maps captions into the very same space, trained on four hundred million image and caption pairs.

7
00:00:25.033 --> 00:00:27.503
Training uses a contrastive objective.

8
00:00:27.503 --> 00:00:30.233
Picture a batch as a grid of similarities.

9
00:00:30.533 --> 00:00:33.467
Pull each image's embedding toward its own caption.

10
00:00:33.767 --> 00:00:36.367
And push it away from every other caption in the batch.

11
00:00:37.567 --> 00:00:39.667
After training, something neat happens.

12
00:00:39.967 --> 00:00:44.867
A photo of a dust devil and the phrase a dust devil land at nearly the same point.

13
00:00:45.167 --> 00:00:47.333
An unrelated phrase sits far away.

14
00:00:48.533 --> 00:00:50.433
Two capabilities fall out for free.

15
00:00:50.733 --> 00:00:58.200
Zero-shot classification: embed your candidate labels as sentences, embed the image, and the nearest label wins.

16
00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:00.533
No training on your classes at all.

17
00:01:00.833 --> 00:01:05.900
And cross-modal search: a text query retrieves images by plain nearest neighbor.

18
00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:09.100
Here's zero-shot classification.

19
00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:12.133
Our candidate labels are just three sentences.

20
00:01:12.433 --> 00:01:15.000
We encode the image and the captions together.

21
00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:19.308
A softmax over the similarities tells us which caption fits best.

22
00:01:19.308 --> 00:01:21.467
No training on these classes, ever.

23
00:01:22.667 --> 00:01:24.200
Here's a weekend project.

24
00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:29.933
You embed five thousand Mars rover photos with CLIP and store them in your vector database.

25
00:01:30.233 --> 00:01:33.181
A geologist types dust devil on the horizon.

26
00:01:33.181 --> 00:01:37.200
The text encoder embeds it and nearest neighbor search runs.

27
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:41.267
The top hits are real dust-devil frames nobody ever tagged.

28
00:01:42.467 --> 00:01:43.261
Let's recap.

29
00:01:43.261 --> 00:01:47.433
CLIP trains an image encoder and a text encoder into one space.

30
00:01:47.733 --> 00:01:50.433
A contrastive loss pulls matching pairs together.

31
00:01:50.733 --> 00:01:55.067
That gives zero-shot classification for free, and cross-modal search too.

32
00:01:55.367 --> 00:01:57.467
Next up: vision-language models.
